# B5. The `reported`-vs-`received` counter mapping, which `ring_bench`'s own
# pitfall/003 calls the crate's most expensive lesson.
#
# The original defect fed workload totals through the slot-lifecycle counters,
# so 240 refused records read as 240 leaked slots on a 16-slot ring.
#
# This is the mutant the whole gate exists for. When the round first reinstated
# it, **every test passed**. The crate had 100% line coverage over 298 lines and
# zero defence against its own headline defect, for two reasons. The test named
# after the job runs a candidate whose `reported` and `received` are equal, so
# the mapping is invisible to it. And the one test using the candidate where
# they differ asserted on `Outcome`'s fields rather than on `outcome.stats()`.
# Both mutated lines executed in both tests. Neither test looked at the number
# they produced.
#
# `a_dropnewest_ring_reports_successes_it_did_not_keep` was then given the four
# counter assertions and `in_flight()` on `ContractRing`, and the re-probe went
# red with exactly one failure. That fix is what this mutant now guards. It is
# the only entry here whose green result was ever the true one, which is
# precisely why leaving it to a human habit was this family's own closing
# constraint.

id: B5
crate: ring_bench
file: src/lib.rs
round: 2026-08-28
round-failures: 1
detector: a_dropnewest_ring_reports_successes_it_did_not_keep

--- from
  stats.record_claim( received as u64 );
  stats.record_publish( received as u64 );
  stats.record_consume( received as u64 );
  stats.record_drop( workload.config().overflow(), ( offered - received ) as u64 );
--- to
  stats.record_claim( reported as u64 );      // PROBE ONLY — G12, was `received`
  stats.record_publish( reported as u64 );
  stats.record_consume( reported as u64 );
  stats.record_drop( workload.config().overflow(), ( offered - reported ) as u64 );
--- end
